new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5d21a2b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bench/workers/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+# Worker benchmarks
+
+From the source checkout:
+
+```sh
+node --experimental-strip-types bench/workers/stage2.mjs report.json 12
+```
+
+This is a **real Node worker_threads** harness through a test Web Worker adapter,
+not browser module-worker performance. It compiles complete static artifact
+sets, validates settled values outside the latency timer, and retains full
+submission-snapshot-to-settlement time. No native benchmark pins are changed.
+
+Eight fixture families come from `tests/workers/workload_shapes.bend`: dense
+integer work, materialized integer images, trivial large outputs, cheap large
+input reductions, repeated sparse immutable updates, skewed independent
+candidates, tiny fork storms and sequential chains. The sparse fixture retains
+unchanged subtrees in its ordinary serial path. A sequential chain must not
+produce invented worker forks.
+
+Configurations include legacy sync, off/zero helpers, auto with two/four helpers,
+required-only unannotated work, and paired source-required one/two/four helpers
+using clone versus packed transport. Source require is real policy execution,
+not diagnostic force. Pool capacity is not reserved physical cores or proof that
+all helpers did useful work.
+
+Five warmups precede rotating/interleaved paired repetitions (12 by default,
+8–100 allowed). Cold calls, compilation/import time, raw warm samples, p50/p95,
+paired bootstrap ratio intervals, counters, output size and memory observations
+are separate. The predeclared informative coarse criterion is an upper 95%
+latency-ratio bound at most 0.8 for two coarse fixtures. Tiny-work added-p95 budget
+is 0.5 ms; relative ratios against a few-microsecond baseline can mislead. A
+42-invocation dense/tiny/dense load-shift sequence records actual decisions and
+bounded profile adaptation. Normal execution is never duplicated to collect a
+calibration sample; this explicit benchmark may perform paired runs.
+
+Packed transport must include both encoding and decoding and mandatory snapshots
+in its reported cost. Clone remains the default; retain small-payload losses and
+large-graph gains rather than reporting only the favorable shape. These runs do
+not implement or measure a managed immutable residency cache.
+
+`process.memoryUsage()` reports whole-process RSS including co-resident helper
+pools, plus coordinator-only heap use. It is not isolated per-fixture/per-helper
+memory or portable browser memory. Scheduler retained-byte estimates are not
+hard VM allocation limits. The method is stored in the raw report.
+
+Important negative boundary: repeated cheap operations on a large input graph
+can be vastly slower than legacy sync because the new public call takes a stable
+snapshot. Automatic refusal to dispatch does not remove that boundary cost.
+Sparse updates and large-input reductions are included specifically to retain
+this evidence. More helper threads and packing do not solve immutable ownership.
+
+The older `measure.mjs` remains a four-family **stage-one marked-only** comparison:
+
+```sh
+node --experimental-strip-types bench/workers/measure.mjs stage1-compatible.json 15
+```
+
+It now requests `marked` explicitly, so the new auto default does not silently
+change its frontier. Its `force4` on a binary root still schedules two root
+subtrees; diagnostic force is not a source-level require witness.
+
+Still needed before full performance acceptance: unrestricted production-browser
+cold/warm distributions, multiple browser engines, a constrained physical
+device, better memory attribution and larger sweeps. Use
+`tests/workers/browser_runner.py` for conformance, then repeat equivalent paired
+performance measurements in the production deployment lane. Node results do not
+establish browser/GPU performance.
diff --git a/bench/workers/measure.mjs b/bench/workers/measure.mjs
