@@ -0,0 +1,281 @@
+# JavaScript Web Worker libraries
+
+This private downstream backend implements the stage-two source-policy,
+adaptive scheduling and packed-transport feature set on Bend **2.0.27**, commit
+`d37909174ebd664338ae3194799a9e0899dedd51`. It remains **experimental**:
+local static-module browser acceptance is established on Windows, but complete
+cross-device performance and published hosting acceptance are open.
+Implementation is not a promise that a particular workload gets faster.
+
+The worker library is an explicit, separate output. Ordinary synchronous JS
+imports and native compilation remain synchronous/native. Auto is the default
+**inside the new output**, not a change to existing applications' APIs.
+
+Read [the contracts](WEB_WORKERS_CONTRACTS.md) and [stage-two maintenance and
+performance notes](WEB_WORKERS_STAGE2.md) before deploying it. These are backend
+engineering contracts, not new theorems checked by Bend's formal core.
+
+## Build and test
+
+From the source checkout, using Node with TypeScript stripping (tested with
+Node 22.16.0):
+
+```sh
+node --experimental-strip-types gates/workers.mjs test
+node --experimental-strip-types gates/workers.mjs compile \
+  tests/workers/policies.bend out/policies require_tree,never_tree required-only strict
+node --experimental-strip-types gates/workers.mjs demo out/worker-demo
+python -m http.server 8080 --directory out/worker-demo
+```
+
+Output directories must be new or empty. Keep each generated artifact set
+together when hosting it. The demo offers an explicit required-region run and
+repeated unmarked automatic runs; it is not a performance certification.
+
+The local compiler gate accepts:
+
+```text
+compile file.bend outdir export1,export2 [auto|required-only|off|marked] [strict|permissive]
+```
+
+A Bun CLI route is also present:
+
+```sh
+bun bend2/main.ts tests/workers/policies.bend \
+  --web-workers=required-only --web-policy=strict \
+  --web-exports=require_tree,never_tree -o out/policies-cli
+```
+
+`--web-workers` alone means `auto`. `--web-workers=required-only` defaults to
+strict enforcement unless `--web-policy=permissive` is supplied. Auto/off default
+to permissive. `--web-policy` without the worker output is rejected; use
+`--web-workers=off --web-policy=strict` to diagnose a disabled worker library.
+The CLI does not add a separate `--web-workers=strict` alias.
+
+The pinned Bun 1.4.2 CLI was exercised on Windows. The HTML route accepts an
+explicit worker library, leaving ordinary `.bend` imports synchronous:
+
+```sh
+bun bend2/main.ts page.html --web-library worker.bend \
+  --web-exports=entry --web-workers=required-only --web-policy=strict -o out/site
+```
+
+The page's external module script imports `bend:workers`. The bundler maps
+that opt-in to a static `workers/<program-id>/index.mjs` and copies all five
+modules as one content-bound set. Inline module-script bodies are not compiled
+by Bun 1.4.2; use an external `src=` module. The output destination must be
+new or empty, and a failed build does not publish a partial set. The focused
+`tests/workers/html_bundle.mjs` checks the ordinary HTML path, option
+exclusions, nested static URLs, and an actual Chrome required-helper witness.
+
+## Source policies
+
+| Call | Worker-enabled JS interpretation |
+| --- | --- |
+| `f(args)` | Automatic candidate selection over existing independent parallel lets. |
+| `f!(args)` | Same automatic policy; retains the native GPU mark and the optional marked-only test-region hint. |
+| `f@(args)` | Require real computation in a helper, ignoring profitability. |
+| `f@4(args)` | Require, with at most four participating helpers in this region. |
+| `f~(args)` | Run this call and its descendants serially in the current executor. |
+| `f!@(args)`, `f!@4(args)`, `f!~(args)` | Combine the existing native mark with the corresponding web policy. |
+
+Only named, saturated calls carry the new directives. The canonical order is
+optional `!`, then `@` with an optional positive decimal cap or `~`, then `(`.
+The suffix/count/parenthesis must be contiguous. Duplicates, mixed policies,
+reordered suffixes, partial or computed callees, zero/negative/fractional caps,
+and a cap on never are errors. Legacy unannotated syntax and bare-bang behavior
+retain their original rules. Template `~` in an argument position is separate.
+
+Call arguments are evaluated **under the caller's policy**. The callee's policy
+starts at body entry and ends at return. Thus `f~(g@(x))` may dispatch `g` while
+preparing `f`'s argument; `f` itself cannot dispatch. Conversely, helper work in
+an argument does not satisfy `f@`'s requirement. Put preparation into a helper
+body when it needs to be part of the region.
+
+Never is a serial island, not a request to move back to the page or coordinator.
+Inside an already remote ancestor it stays in that helper. A nested bang cannot
+escape it. A require reached inside never is a conflict: strict rejects,
+permissive records a warning and honors never. Path-dependent conflicts are
+checked at actual region entry. Returning from an island restores the enclosing
+policy. An ancestor is not made ineligible merely because it contains never.
+
+A successful required region has a real task execution witness. Idle workers,
+handshakes, unrelated siblings and argument preparation do not count. A supported
+fork-free call can be sent whole to one helper; nested requirements already
+executing there coalesce, without another pool. For a conditional call whose
+selected branch returns without witnessing its requirement, the coordinator can
+perform one whole-call required fallback. That may repeat the pure local prefix;
+it is explicitly traced, not hidden profiling or a retry after a worker failure.
+
+Caps narrow: `min(source cap, enclosing cap, session cap)`. They bound distinct
+participating helpers, not reserved processors, exact helper count or minimum
+parallelism. A one-helper session can satisfy `@4`. Nested caps and concurrent
+root calls share the one bounded session pool.
+
+## Scheduling and enforcement are separate
+
+| Scope | Outside require | Inside require | Inside never |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `auto` (default) | Eligible marked and unmarked parallel lets may dispatch. | Profitability cannot veto legal requested execution. | No further dispatch. |
+| `required-only` | Local, including a bare bang. | Region-wide permission; leaves need not repeat `@`. | No further dispatch. |
+| `off` | Local. | Unfulfillable; selected enforcement applies. | Local. |
+| `marked` (development compatibility) | Consider only supported bang regions. | Explicit require still applies. | No further dispatch. |
+
+Strict statically unsupported/disabled requirements are compile errors. At
+runtime, strict unavailable, over-budget or nonfinite-input requirements reject
+without publishing a Bend result. Permissive requirements take a documented
+local route with a bounded site/reason diagnostic. Task crashes, invalid replies,
+protocol failures and invalid host shapes are errors in either enforcement mode;
+they are not ordinary capability fallback. Never is always binding.
+
+Requirements in public legacy synchronous JS output warn because that target
+cannot honor them. `Comp.js_lib(..., {policy: 'strict'})` rejects them instead.
+Internal serial clones have known placement/context; they are not public exports
+entitled to discard requirements. Native targets ignore web-only policies/caps
+while retaining the original native bang. Typing and definitional equality ignore
+all scheduling metadata; the formal core and erasure rules are unchanged.
+
+## Compiler and packaging APIs
+
+`Comp.js_worker_lib(book, roots, {mode, policy})` is distinct from `Comp.js_lib`.
+It requires a checked, owned, hole-free book. Compilation remains serialized;
+module-level compiler memo tables are not used concurrently.
+
+```ts
+import {load_worker_js, write_worker_files} from './bend2/main.ts';
+
+const build = await load_worker_js('my_module.bend', ['entry'], {
+  mode: 'auto',
+  policy: 'strict',
+});
+write_worker_files(build, 'out/my_module');
+console.log(build.diagnostics);
+```
+
+The compiler returns five deployment files: `index.mjs`, content-named program,
+worker and runtime modules, and `manifest.json`. Worker URLs are module-relative.
+Program/schema/runtime content is bound into the generated identity. The local
+gate additionally emits `serial.mjs` and `diagnostics.json` as **test/oracle
+sidecars**, not runtime dependencies. Do not publish the serial sidecar as a
+substitute policy-enforcing API.
+
+`write_worker_files` refuses nonempty destinations and rolls back files it
+created if a write fails. It does not deploy, publish or update applications.
+The compiler-owned `bend2/web_runtime.js` must ship beside Base in the installed
+compiler resources. Release-installer integration lives outside this checkout
+and remains unverified; do not assume building one compiler executable embeds
+all necessary resources.
+
+## Host API and lifetime
+
+```js
+import {createSession} from './out/my_module/index.mjs';
+
+const session = createSession({workers: 4, transport: 'clone'});
+try {
+  const value = await session.call('entry', [input]);
+  console.log(value, session.stats(), session.diagnostics());
+} finally {
+  session.close();
+}
+```
+
+Supply the exact saturated **live** argument vector. Erased arguments are absent.
+Unlike the legacy wrapper, this API does not curry or partially apply exports.
+A session inherits both scope and enforcement from its compiled manifest;
+when overriding the profile at runtime, set both `mode` and `policy` explicitly.
+
+`submit(name, args, {signal})` returns `{id, promise, cancel}`; `call` returns that
+Promise directly. `cancel(id)`, the handle's `cancel()` and AbortSignal invalidate
+an invocation. `close()` is idempotent, rejects outstanding work, removes
+listeners and terminates owned helpers. A busy synchronous helper cannot handle
+a cancellation message before returning; cancellation prevents publication but
+does not promise immediate CPU reclamation. There is no automatic restart loop.
+
+`warmup()` explicitly creates/handshakes the pool and rejects if helpers are
+unavailable. Ordinary auto execution may instead fall back before dispatch.
+Neither module import nor session construction starts helpers. `workerURL` and
+`workerFactory` allow explicit hosting/test integration; they do not bypass the
+handshake, allowlisted function table or input/schema checks.
+
+`stats()`, `diagnostics()` and `profile()` return bounded diagnostic copies.
+`trace: true` enables a capped 2,048-event placement log for tests. Source-site
+warnings are deduplicated within a 128-entry window. Profiles are local to a
+session/program build; there is no persistent storage or telemetry.
+
+## Inputs, transport and numeric boundary
+
+Supported wire types are concrete first-order U32, 48-bit-bounded Nat (BigInt),
+Bool, Unicode scalar Char/String, finite F32, and supported recursive ADTs with
+those live fields. Functions, handles, mutable Bend Array boundaries, unresolved
+polymorphic schemas and reachable unsafe/foreign computations are not made safe
+by a policy marker. Closure/Array values held only in a coordinator continuation
+are a different case and have local-continuation tests.
+
+Supported public input graphs are validated and copied synchronously inside
+`submit`, before suspension. Cycles, accessors, unexpected fields, incompatible
+DAG schema aliases, invalid primitive values, invalid Unicode and configured
+limits reject. Host input objects are never frozen or detached. There is no
+promise to preserve external JS reference identity. Unsupported public boundary
+signatures take a wholly synchronous compatibility route (live-arity check and
+original execution), with no suspension or recursive worker dispatch; callers
+must still obey their original Bend value contract.
+
+`transport: 'clone'` uses structured clone and is the default. `transport:
+'packed'` selects a schema-driven iterative little-endian buffer encoding, with
+constructor IDs/references, UTF-8 strings and primitive fields. Only buffers
+allocated by the runtime are transferred. Decoding is bounded and rejects
+invalid references, truncation, malformed strings and trailing bytes. Sharing
+inside one accepted message is preserved; cross-message identity and managed
+immutable residency are **not** implemented. Packing is optional because small
+values can be slower after encoding/decoding.
+
+F32 support is deliberately finite-at-dispatch. The original expression tree,
+`Math.fround` placement and arithmetic implementations are retained, including
+signed zero. Host nonfinite F32 inputs may use a local route but cannot be sent
+as finite-only tasks. Strict require on such an input fails. A remote nonfinite
+result fails with an explicit unsupported-result error; it is not silently
+canonicalized or retried locally. Bit-sensitive F32 constructors/matches and
+unapproved numeric paths remain task-ineligible even when a task's external
+types are U32. No NaN-payload or native/JS/cross-browser bit-equivalence claim is
+made. See the numeric audit in the stage-two notes.
+
+## Bounds and tuning
+
+The default helper cap is `min(4, max(1, hardwareConcurrency - 1))`, using an
+advisory hardware value (fallback 2), and can be set from 0 to 32. Defaults also
+include 64 jobs per invocation, 128 ready jobs, 32 concurrent roots, 128 profiles,
+64 MiB retained scheduler data and 32 MiB queued data. Wire defaults are 200,000
+traversal nodes, depth 256, 16 MiB estimated bytes and 4 Mi UTF-16 string units.
+These are accepted-data/scheduler bounds, **not hard worker-process allocation
+limits**. A pure computation can allocate before its output is checked.
+
+Automatic selection uses actual local/body and dispatched observations, not
+duplicate foreground executions. Bounded per-site/structural-size/frontier-depth
+profiles include variance, output estimates, queue/movement cost and helper
+makespan. Unknown, tiny or unstable work stays local. Hysteresis and eight-sample
+rolling histories avoid one sample determining the route forever. Defaults:
+`minSamples: 2`, `minWorkMs: 4`, `margin: 1.4`, `assumedRoundTripMs: 2`.
+
+Deeper coordinator expansion defaults to `frontierDepth: 2` and
+`maxExpansions: 16`; the marked-only compatibility mode defaults to depth zero.
+It only follows existing source independence, shares the invocation budget and
+leaves helper subtrees synchronous. Sequential chains are not split. Require can
+intentionally be slower; `diagnosticForce` is a test-only profitability override,
+not source require and not permission to bypass safety, never or hard budgets.
+
+Snapshot, inspection and coordinator costs remain real even when auto chooses
+local. In particular, large immutable inputs with very cheap work can be much
+slower than the legacy sync API. Keep that API for unsuitable call boundaries;
+use the benchmark harness rather than assuming that more workers help.
+
+## Evidence and remaining acceptance
+
+The delivered evidence includes local compiler/runtime tests, a 639-file
+baseline differential check, a native Clang policy smoke test, real Node helper
+measurements and real Chromium runtime tests. The Chromium tests that ran use an
+explicitly labelled **classic Blob test adaptation**. Production same-origin
+module hosting was blocked by the environment's browser policy; that is not a
+passing module/CSP/packaging result. Firefox, WebKit, constrained-device/browser
+performance, offline deployment, the Bun HTML route and release packaging remain
+open. See the delivery's `HANDOFF.md` and evidence index for exact receipts.
