@@ -0,0 +1,295 @@
+# Stage-two implementation and maintenance notes
+
+Source baseline: **Bend 2.0.27**, commit
+`d37909174ebd664338ae3194799a9e0899dedd51`. This is the maintained downstream
+continuation of the stage-one archive, not an upstream release or a claim that
+all design acceptance gates are complete. The source API is documented in
+[WEB_WORKERS.md](WEB_WORKERS.md); the invariants are in
+[WEB_WORKERS_CONTRACTS.md](WEB_WORKERS_CONTRACTS.md).
+
+## Implemented scope
+
+The new worker output defaults to automatic selection over eligible **existing
+parallel lets**. It adds source require/never and caps, strict/permissive
+execution, bounded deeper coordinator decomposition, per-site adaptive costs,
+optional typed packed transport and finite-F32 task boundaries. It retains
+original synchronous serial task functions and static multi-artifact emission.
+
+It does not infer independence from arbitrary sequential source, create nested
+helper pools, serialize closures, introduce shared memory, change IO.fork, add
+browser GPU execution, make Arrays/handles transferable, or implement a managed
+immutable residency cache. Packed transport is the independently measured
+locality/transport mechanism selected from section 7.4 of the supplied design.
+It does not eliminate root snapshots or promise cross-invocation object identity.
+
+## S2-01: syntax, policy and resource assertions
+
+### Explicit downstream parser permission
+
+The original AGENTS instruction protects the human-written `bend2/bend.ts`.
+This requested language-facing patch adds a narrow, dated exception in AGENTS:
+Ref metadata, parser/printer support, metadata-preserving conversions/template
+instantiation and the rewrite-delimiter fix. The exception does not authorize
+changes to typing/equality/termination, erasure, Base or `bend.lean`. These other
+core rules remain unchanged. The parser file's original CRLF format is retained.
+
+### Metadata audit
+
+`TermOf.Ref` and the `Ref` constructor add optional `web` (`require`/`never`) and
+`webMax` fields separately from `b`. Conditional construction leaves ordinary
+unannotated Ref shapes unchanged. The copying sites in higher/lower conversion,
+normalization/reference handling and template/inference substitution preserve
+those fields. Operations that return an existing Ref keep the object itself.
+The source printer emits the canonical native-bang/worker-policy order.
+
+`parse_term_ops` only treats the new suffix positions as named saturated calls.
+Legacy bang-only parsing is retained, including its diagnostic path for a
+computed/bare mark. Prefix function types, annotations, `!=`, template parameters
+and arguments keep their separate syntax. The `%` rewrite branch recognizes a
+named binder before general term parsing can steal `%name@(proof)` as a worker
+call. A grouped term remains the disambiguation for a worker expression there.
+New fixtures test that exact parenthesized proof, accepted/rejected combinations,
+full call printer/reparser round trips and metadata-preserving conversion.
+
+The typed core's comparison ignores policies. A source proof checks equality
+between `identity@(x)` and `identity~(x)` and requires no helper to check the
+proof. Dead annotations neither manufacture tasks nor cause strict-off errors.
+No parallel effect or new logical term constructor is introduced.
+
+### Compiler propagation and execution
+
+Compiler `Call` records retain policy/count/source span. Worker analysis keeps
+eligibility, reachable marks, reachable policies/requirements, potential forks
+and suspension separate. Original serial definitions remain available alongside
+selective generator coordinators. Named call steps are emitted after argument
+preparation and carry their own policy metadata. Intrinsic calls only become
+named policy steps at their explicitly annotated sites; another annotated use of
+an intrinsic must not accidentally make ordinary arithmetic a helper task.
+
+The coordinator uses explicit region/frame records. A region tracks its owning
+invocation, cap, participants and witness. Arguments run before entry. Region
+permission never leaks after return or to another root. Never passes a serial
+context into the original synchronous body or the synchronous policy interpreter.
+Helpers run the same serial interpreter when a body contains directives; it can
+check nested requirements/never without creating a Promise or another pool.
+
+Only a real successful computation reply witnesses coordinator-owned require.
+Nested calls already executing in a helper can coalesce, provided they are not
+under never. Fork-free eligible calls can be sent whole. Potential-fork calls
+that take a witness-free branch perform at most one explicit whole-call fallback
+at region completion. That fallback can repeat a pure prefix already executed
+locally; it is recorded as `required_whole_call`. It is not a timing sample,
+worker-crash retry or permission to duplicate effects.
+
+Unsupported/disabled explicit require has target/enforcement diagnostics.
+Runtime conflicts follow the actual executed dynamic path, avoiding rejection
+merely because an untaken branch contains a conflict. Strict runtime closure
+cases with embedded requirements are conservatively unsupported rather than
+silently erasing policy. Native output ignores web metadata and retains bang.
+
+## S2-02: bounded adaptation and deeper frontiers
+
+The coordinator measures real local body durations and actual dispatched
+subtrees. Profile keys combine source site, function IDs, logarithmic accepted
+byte/node buckets and **frontier depth**. Numeric seeds are not assumed to be
+work size. The depth component prevents observations of a coarse recursive fork
+from being polluted by timings for its smaller expanded descendants.
+
+Each profile retains at most eight recent body-duration samples and bounded
+child samples, with mean, variance, minimum, output estimate and enable/disable
+history. Profile count is bounded (128 by default), session/build-local and
+nonpersistent. A load/JIT regime is forgotten through real new observations,
+not a duplicate foreground calibration run. Tests cover bounded history,
+recovery and defensive copies of the diagnostic API.
+
+Prediction combines conservative local work with a largest-processing-time
+helper assignment, child variance, queued work, measured round trips, movement,
+receiver/output and root snapshot estimates. Two consecutive favorable or
+unfavorable observations provide hysteresis. Unknown/tiny/high-variance work is
+local. The model is a heuristic, not an oracle: scalar shape buckets deliberately
+cannot distinguish every early-return/data-dependent cost. Short sampling
+windows and cold JIT can keep otherwise suitable work local; recorded load-shift
+measurements show both learning and relearning.
+
+Deeper frontier expansion is coordinator-owned, follows only existing siblings,
+and shares invocation job, region, expansion, queue and byte budgets. Default
+maximum additional depth is two and expansion count is sixteen. A small pool
+may have modest slack (for example eight coarse jobs for four helpers), not one
+worker for every recursive node. A frontier token is consumed by actual
+subdivision/dispatch so unrelated cheap continuation joins are not shipped as
+extra coarse work. Helpers always execute complete serial subtrees.
+
+When a parent is expanded, its profile is updated from actual descendant body
+work rather than left permanently stale. Local timing excludes redundant
+inspection of generated results; otherwise a cheap large result can look
+compute-heavy and cause exactly the wrong scheduling choice. `measureTrusted`
+provides bounded size/accounting without repeating expensive host descriptor
+validation at each intermediate join. Public snapshots and unchecked replies
+still use the untrusted validation path.
+
+## S2-03: typed packed transport
+
+Clone remains the default. `transport: 'packed'` is negotiated per session and
+uses flat runtime-owned ArrayBuffers transferred through postMessage. There is
+no caller-owned buffer detachment or ambient cache keyed by mutable host objects.
+
+Wire magic is `0x32574442`. Encoding is schema-guided and little-endian: U32,
+48-bit-bounded Nat in a 64-bit field, Bool, finite float32, UTF-8 Char/String, and
+ADT constructor ordinals with numeric references to already established nodes.
+The encoder and decoder are iterative. Sharing within one acyclic graph is
+preserved; cross-schema aliasing, cycles/forward references, malformed tags,
+invalid strings, truncated/oversized/trailing data reject. The decoder validates,
+reconstructs and accounts in one traversal. It does not assume that a peer's
+schema claim makes arbitrary bytes valid.
+
+Pack/decode time and bytes are visible in counters and full invocation latency.
+Packed buffers have their own allocation/copy cost, and helper execution can
+allocate beyond accepted input sizes. Scheduler limits are not allocator quotas.
+There is no residency ID lifecycle, missing-reference reupload, managed-value
+ownership API or returned canonical-subtree reuse: those are future independent
+work, not claims hidden behind this codec.
+
+## S2-04: automatic default and numeric expansion
+
+Only `js_worker_lib` changes to automatic-by-default. `required-only` defaults
+strict when compiled; auto/off default permissive, with independent overrides.
+The development `marked` mode retains the old shallow-frontier behavior for
+historical comparisons. Ordinary `.bend` imports remain synchronous.
+
+F32 wire schemas admit finite, exactly representable F32 JS Numbers. Signed zero
+survives both transport routes. Eligibility reviews reachable numeric operations,
+including bit-sensitive constructors/matches and intermediate values behind
+integer signatures, rather than only banning a named conversion function.
+Ordinary finite arithmetic/transcendental/comparison and numeric-conversion
+paths use an explicit intrinsic whitelist. Dynamic F32 bit constructors,
+representation matches and nonfinite literal patterns stay task-ineligible.
+
+Host F32 shape validation can recognize nonfinite Numbers for the local route;
+an invocation carrying one is conservatively blocked from dispatch. A strict
+require then fails; permissive records a waiver. A remote nonfinite result is an
+explicit failure, not a payload-preserving result, silent canonicalization or
+unannounced serial recomputation. Existing NaN limitations, dependent bit-tag
+behavior and native/JS differences are not fixed by adding workers.
+
+Tests cover clone/packed finite values, positive/negative zero, nonfinite host
+inputs, nonfinite remote results, and bit-sensitive F32 computations with integer
+boundaries. Same-engine serial/worker tests do not establish cross-engine or
+native equality.
+
+## Reproducible validation
+
+```sh
+# New and inherited runtime/compiler tests; real Node helper threads included.
+node --experimental-strip-types gates/workers.mjs test
+
+# Explicit baseline checkout required; no upstream cluster access.
+node --experimental-strip-types tests/workers/regression_matrix.mjs \
+  /path/to/pristine-bend regression.json
+
+# Eight positive/negative families, paired raw samples and load shifts.
+node --experimental-strip-types bench/workers/stage2.mjs benchmark.json 12
+
+# Static module artifacts and production browser attempt.
+node --experimental-strip-types gates/workers.mjs demo out/browser
+python tests/workers/browser_runner.py --artifacts out/browser \
+  --browser chromium --output browser-modules.json
+
+# Separately labelled runtime experiment; NOT production module acceptance.
+python tests/workers/browser_runner.py --artifacts out/browser \
+  --browser chromium --blob-classic --output browser-classic.json
+```
+
+The differential matrix launches isolated revisions with the same V8
+`--stack-size=4096`; a 3,000-deep upstream rewrite proof was host-stack/JIT
+sensitive under the default stack. It compares parsing/checking, holes/open
+counts and normalized JS/native-C emission hashes for compile fixtures. It does
+not execute every upstream program or prove native/device behavior. The supplied
+receipt has 639 unchanged outcomes, including 119 compile fixtures. Separate
+exact output pins and a Clang-compiled policy smoke test provide narrower
+additional evidence.
+
+The original delivered local suite had 106 passing tests. A real Chromium 144.0.7559.96
+classic-worker adaptation passes 19 checks, including page and existing-worker
+callers, helpers, policies, packing and F32. Production module-hosting navigation
+was blocked by managed browser policy (`ERR_BLOCKED_BY_ADMINISTRATOR`), not by a
+recorded semantic failure of the generated program. This adaptation must not be
+used to claim production module, CSP, MIME, static/offline or bundler acceptance.
+Firefox/WebKit, browser/device performance and unrestricted deployment remain
+open. The Node-only TypeScript check still reports two baseline/environment
+issues; no clean full Bun typecheck or Bun CLI run is claimed for that delivery.
+
+### Later Rift Chess Windows integration (September 25)
+
+The downstream 001+002+worker rebase passes 107 tests (including the pinned
+Bun HTML bundle), and a fresh replay matches its canonical compiler source
+hash. Static ESM module workers under a nested HTTP path pass the 19 functional
+checks in Chrome 153, Edge 153, Firefox 155 and WebKit 26.6; missing/corrupt
+workers, CSP denial, mixed builds and lifecycle have separate checks. WebKit
+executes correct JavaScript with a `text/plain` worker response, unlike the
+other engines, so no universal MIME rejection is claimed. A relocated
+compiler-resource copy includes `web_runtime.js` and emits a library. The
+639-fixture matrix still has no differences after HTML integration. The
+automatic coarse performance criterion remains unmet; explicit required
+helpers have measured desktop browser wins, but no claim is made for all
+devices or cheap large-input calls. The Rift project records exact patch
+bytes, browser receipts and limits in
+`bend2/toolchain-patches/004-web-workers/LOCAL_RECEIPT.md`.
+
+## Performance interpretation and remaining work
+
+The measured stage-two harness retains eight families: dense scalar reduction,
+materialized procedural image, cheap large output, cheap large-input reduction,
+repeated sparse immutable update, skewed independent candidates, tiny forks and
+a sequential chain. It compares legacy sync, zero-helper/off, auto with two/four
+helpers, required-only unmarked exclusion, required one/two/four helpers and
+required packed transport. Full call latency includes submission snapshots.
+
+Real Node measurements demonstrate coarse required-helper speedups and cases
+where packing reduces large-graph invocation cost. Small packed values can lose,
+so clone is not replaced by default. Auto learning/relearning is observable in
+the separate load-shift sequence, while conservative short-window decisions and
+cold-start/JIT outliers remain. The sequential chain is never invented into
+parallel work. Required execution may intentionally be slower on cheap jobs.
+
+Large cheap immutable inputs remain a major limit: public snapshotting and
+materialization can cost tens of milliseconds where the legacy synchronous
+operation takes a fraction of one. Packed messages do not remove that cost, and
+correct local auto decisions do not make the new boundary cheap. A future
+explicit managed immutable ownership/residency API is the appropriate experiment;
+a WeakMap keyed by unconstrained caller objects would not be safe.
+
+Full design acceptance therefore remains open, especially production browser
+transport/packaging, multi-device performance distributions and large-data
+snapshot regressions. Do not present the tested feature set as all section 7.7
+exit criteria having passed. Preserve current semantics while evaluating those
+next steps; do not loosen safety or hide negative fixtures to obtain a win.
+
+## Rebase/rollback map
+
+The delivery contains a stage-one patch and a stage-two patch applied in that
+order against the pinned baseline, plus the complete resulting source. Retaining
+only stage one removes the new parser policies and stage-two runtime; using the
+legacy compiler output avoids the worker API altogether. Worker scope `off` is
+a diagnostic serial route, not permission to erase strict requirements.
+
+On an upstream update, audit Ref copies, parser postfix/rewrite positions,
+erasure, saturation, template specialization, JS constructor/numeric layouts,
+trampolines, compiler memo tables, loader resources and planned JS continuations.
+A clean text apply is not a semantic rebase. The checked-in local regression
+matrix and stage-two fixtures should run against both revisions before promotion.
+
+### Git checkout versus uploaded archive line endings
+
+The supplied Windows source archive has CRLF text files, while the pinned Git
+objects use LF. The complete source snapshot preserves the uploaded spelling of
+untouched files (and the parser/AGENTS convention); replayable patches use the
+canonical Git-LF baseline. Replay comparison ignores only CRLF/LF spelling for
+UTF-8 text and requires exact bytes for binary files.
+
+The native emitter embeds effect-file source verbatim. Therefore an LF checkout
+can have a different exact native-C output hash from the CRLF archive despite
+identical compiler behavior. `oracle_hashes.json` retains the original archive
+pins and adds `nativeLF` pins generated with an independent pristine Git-LF
+compiler/checker, never the downstream compiler. Tests accept only those two
+exact native variants, not arbitrary normalized output. The JS pins are identical
+and retained unchanged. The final patch replay validates the canonical LF lane.
